<% content_for(:title) { "Features — Digibujo" } %>

<div class="features--container">


  <article class="features--card" data-colour="1">
    <h2>Rapid logging</h2>
    <p>
      A dock on the daily log: pick Task, Note, Event, or Voice and dump the
      thought. Escape restores the picker. No nested “create inside X” maze.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Four bullets, one flow</h2>
    <p>
      Task, Note, Event, and Voice — tuned to what you’re capturing, same
      composer language for all four.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>Today’s page</h2>
    <p>
      One date, one list — the analog daily log on the web. Flip days, postpone
      what’s not for today, and keep moving.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="4">
    <h2>Monthly spread</h2>
    <p>
      A calendar month with days, habits, and an unplanned column for undated
      items. Compose from a dialog right on the spread.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="5">
    <h2>Future log</h2>
    <p>
      Park months that don’t have a spread yet. Stay forward-looking without
      forcing a date onto every thought.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Capture, then curate</h2>
    <p>
      Brain-dump first, organise later. Daily logs, projects, and collections
      keep the noise navigable without slowing you down.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="7">
    <h2>Migrate, don’t rewrite</h2>
    <p>
      Later, Save, Archive, Complete, Pin — move bullets instead of rewriting
      them. A › marker shows what’s already migrated.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="8">
    <h2>Keyboard-first</h2>
    <p>
      Every common intent — compose, navigate, pop, collect, complete, archive —
      has a hotkey, with hints surfaced inline.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="1">
    <h2>Review workspace</h2>
    <p>
      Triage the last seven days of bullets that still need a decision.
      Desktop: collections, inbox, and upcoming days with drag-and-drop. Mark
      all reviewed when you’re done looking.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Postpone without guilt</h2>
    <p>
      Push a bullet to tomorrow — or any day — and keep going. Decisions
      deferred, not lost.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>Collections as notebooks</h2>
    <p>
      Named, coloured, icon’d buckets. Collect from the timeline or review,
      then export a collection as HTML when you want it out.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="4">
    <h2># projects</h2>
    <p>
      Tag projects inline while typing notes. Pills stick to the
      bullet; open a project page to see everything related.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="5">
    <h2>Pinned dock</h2>
    <p>
      Pin bullets, collections, or projects into one always-reachable
      dock — popover on desktop, full page on mobile.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Archive soft, purge later</h2>
    <p>
      Soft-hide what’s done, browse archives when you need them, and let
      retention clean expired items after about thirty days. Pins stay out of
      the sweep.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="7">
    <h2>Trackers</h2>
    <p>
      Habits, not bullets — pick the days of the week, mark them off, then check
      streaks on a ninety-day heatmap.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="8">
    <h2>Search and command menu</h2>
    <p>
      Find bullets fast and jump to Home, Daylog, Monthly, or Review without
      leaving the keyboard.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="1">
    <h2>Publish what you want</h2>
    <p>
      Publish individual bullets to share them on the internet. Each one gets a
      short code and a clean public view — no login to read.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Appearance that stays quiet</h2>
    <p>
      Home tint themes — default, warm, cool, nature, cheese. Atmosphere without
      a settings rabbit hole.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>Outlining</h2>
    <p>
      Like TaskPaper — but no special headers. Any bullet can outline others:
      just indent and the tree builds itself. Navigate it with the keyboard,
      fold the noise away, focus on one branch.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="4">
    <h2>CLI &amp; agent integration</h2>
    <p>
      Drive Digibujo from a terminal or from an AI agent. The same bullets,
      projects, and collections — programmable from outside the browser.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="5">
    <h2>Hooks</h2>
    <p>
      Drop notes and tasks into your Pending inbox from Zapier, agents, or your
      own scripts — one URL per integration.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Self-host, open source</h2>
    <p>
      Ready for self-deployment with Kamal. Your data stays in your own SQLite
      databases — no Redis, no telemetry. Self-host freely under the O’Saasy
      license.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="7">
    <h2>Crafted with love</h2>
    <p>
      Built on Rails 8 and Hotwire, SQLite and Solid Queue under the hood,
      Kamal to ship it.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
